\title{Adjacent-Vertex-Distinguishing Total Colourings of\\ Central Graphs of Regular Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For a finite simple graph $G$, the \emph{central graph} $\Cent(G)$ is obtained by
subdividing every edge of $G$ exactly once and then joining every pair of vertices
that are non-adjacent in $G$. We study the \emph{adjacent-vertex-distinguishing
(AVD) total chromatic number} $\chita(\Cent(G))$ when $G$ is regular. A guiding
conjecture predicts $\chita(\Cent(G)) = d+3$ for every $d$-regular non-complete
graph $G$ (with $\chita(\Cent(K_{d+1})) = d+2$). We show this conjecture is false
in general. The key structural fact is that every original vertex of $\Cent(G)$ has
degree $|V(G)|-1$, so any two vertices non-adjacent in $G$ become adjacent
maximum-degree vertices in $\Cent(G)$. A general obstruction --- two adjacent
vertices of equal maximum degree cannot be distinguished with only $\Delta+1$
colours --- then yields the unconditional lower bound
$\chita(\Cent(G)) \ge |V(G)| + 1$ for every non-complete $G$. Writing $n=|V(G)|$,
for regular non-complete $G$ we have $n \ge d+2$, so this bound meets $d+3$ only at
the boundary $n=d+2$ and strictly exceeds it whenever $n>d+2$; the pentagon $C_5$
is an explicit counterexample with $\chita(\Cent(C_5)) \ge 6 > 5$. In the complete
case the original (maximum-degree) vertices form an independent set, which is
precisely why the argument does not apply. Collecting both cases we conjecture the
unified value $\chita(\Cent(G)) = n+1$ for all regular $G$, and confirm it on small
instances, e.g.\ $\chita(\Cent(K_3)) = 4$. We prove the lower-bound half in full and
isolate the missing upper-bound constructions as the remaining open problem.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A \emph{total colouring} of a graph $H$ assigns colours to both vertices and edges
so that (i) adjacent vertices, (ii) adjacent edges, and (iii) incident vertex--edge
pairs all receive distinct colours; the least number of colours is the
\emph{total chromatic number} $\chit(H)$. The Behzad--Vizing Total Colouring
Conjecture, $\chit(H) \le \Delta(H)+2$, remains open.

A refinement adds a distinguishing requirement. For a total colouring, the
\emph{colour set} of a vertex $w$ is
\[
S(w) = \{\text{colour of } w\} \cup \{\text{colour of } e : e \text{ incident to } w\}.
\]
A total colouring is \emph{adjacent-vertex-distinguishing (AVD)} if
$S(u) \ne S(v)$ for every edge $uv$; the \emph{AVD total chromatic number}
$\chita(H)$ is the least number of colours in such a colouring. Since the colours on
a vertex and its incident edges are pairwise distinct, $|S(w)| = \deg(w)+1$ always,
so the distinguishing constraint is most restrictive between neighbours of equal
degree.

The \emph{central graph} $\Cent(G)$ is obtained from $G$ by subdividing each edge
once and adding an edge between each pair of vertices non-adjacent in $G$. The
following conjecture has circulated for AVD total colourings of central graphs of
regular graphs.

\begin{conjecture}[Guiding]
For every $d$-regular graph $G$ with $d \ge 2$ that is not complete,
$\chita(\Cent(G)) = d+3$; and $\chita(\Cent(K_{d+1})) = d+2$.
\end{conjecture}

We develop a self-contained theory of total and AVD total colourings, compute the
degree structure of $\Cent(G)$, and show the guiding conjecture fails once
$|V(G)| > d+2$, replacing it with a conjecture governed by $n=|V(G)|$.

\section{Definitions}

Graphs are finite and simple; $V(H)$, $E(H)$, $\deg_H$, $\Delta(H)$ denote the
vertex set, edge set, degree function and maximum degree.

\begin{definition}[Total graph]
The \emph{total graph} $\Tot(H)$ has vertex set $V(H) \sqcup E(H)$. Two elements are
adjacent when: both are vertices adjacent in $H$; or both are edges sharing an
endpoint; or one is a vertex $x$ and the other an edge $e$ with $x \in e$. A total
colouring of $H$ is a proper colouring of $\Tot(H)$, and $\chit(H) = \chi(\Tot(H))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Colour set, AVD]
Given a proper colouring $c$ of $\Tot(H)$, the colour set of $w$ is
$S(w) = \{c(w)\} \cup \{c(e) : w \in e\}$. The colouring is AVD if $S(u) \ne S(v)$
for every edge $uv$; $\chita(H)$ is the least number of colours of an AVD total
colouring.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Central graph]
The \emph{central graph} $\Cent(G)$ has vertex set $V(G) \sqcup E(G)$ (\emph{original}
and \emph{subdivision} vertices). Two original vertices $u,w$ are adjacent iff
$u \ne w$ and $uw \notin E(G)$; an original vertex $u$ and a subdivision vertex $e$
are adjacent iff $u \in e$; two subdivision vertices are never adjacent.
\end{definition}

\section{The star clique and total-chromatic lower bound}

The \emph{closed star} of $w$ consists of $w$ together with all edges incident to $w$.

\begin{lemma}[Star clique]
For every vertex $w$, the closed star of $w$ is a clique of $\Tot(H)$ of size
$\deg_H(w)+1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
There are $\deg_H(w)$ edges at $w$, plus $w$; pairwise adjacency in $\Tot(H)$ holds
because $w$ is incident to each of its edges and any two edges at $w$ share $w$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Maximum-degree bound]
$\chit(H) \ge \deg_H(w)+1$ for every $w$; in particular $\chit(H) \ge \Delta(H)+1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Palette saturation]
If a proper colouring of $\Tot(H)$ uses exactly $\deg_H(w)+1$ colours, then $S(w)$ is
the entire palette.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The $\deg_H(w)+1$ closed-star elements are pairwise adjacent, hence receive
pairwise distinct colours; with exactly that many colours available, all occur on
the star, so $S(w)$ is everything.
\end{proof}

\section{The adjacent equal-degree obstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Adjacent equal-degree obstruction]\label{thm:obstruction}
Let $u,v$ be adjacent with $\deg_H(u) = \deg_H(v) = \Delta(H)$. Then no AVD total
colouring uses only $\Delta(H)+1$ colours; equivalently $\chita(H) \ge \Delta(H)+2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose a proper colouring uses exactly $\Delta(H)+1 = \deg_H(u)+1 = \deg_H(v)+1$
colours. By palette saturation applied to $u$ and $v$, both $S(u)$ and $S(v)$ equal
the full palette, so $S(u)=S(v)$; as $u,v$ are adjacent this violates AVD. Hence
at least $\Delta(H)+2$ colours are required.
\end{proof}

\section{Degree structure of the central graph}

Let $n = |V(G)|$.

\begin{theorem}[Subdivision degree]
Every subdivision vertex of $\Cent(G)$ has degree $2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A subdivision vertex $e=\{a,b\}$ is adjacent exactly to its endpoints $a,b$; it is
non-adjacent to all other subdivision vertices and incident to no other original
vertex.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Original-vertex degree]\label{thm:origdeg}
Every original vertex $u$ of $\Cent(G)$ has degree $n-1$; equivalently
$\deg_{\Cent(G)}(u)+1 = n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Partition the $n-1$ vertices $w \ne u$ by adjacency to $u$ in $G$. Each non-neighbour
$w$ gives an original-vertex edge $uw$ of $\Cent(G)$; each neighbour $w$ gives the
subdivision-vertex neighbour $\{u,w\}$. These are distinct and exhaust all
neighbours of $u$, so
$\deg_{\Cent(G)}(u) = (n-1-\deg_G(u)) + \deg_G(u) = n-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $n \ge 3$, $\Delta(\Cent(G)) = n-1$, attained by every original vertex.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Complete-graph independence]\label{thm:indep}
In $\Cent(K_n)$ the original vertices form an independent set.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Any two distinct original vertices are adjacent in $K_n$, hence non-adjacent in
$\Cent(K_n)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Lower bounds for central graphs}

\begin{theorem}[Total lower bound]
For any $G$ with $n = |V(G)| \ge 1$, $\chit(\Cent(G)) \ge n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the maximum-degree bound to an original vertex $u$:
$\chit(\Cent(G)) \ge \deg_{\Cent(G)}(u)+1 = n$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:origdeg}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Main AVD lower bound]\label{thm:main}
If $G$ is not complete --- there exist distinct $a,b$ with $ab \notin E(G)$ --- then
$\chita(\Cent(G)) \ge n+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $ab \notin E(G)$, the original vertices $a,b$ are adjacent in $\Cent(G)$; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:origdeg} both have degree $n-1 = \Delta(\Cent(G))$. Applying
Theorem~\ref{thm:obstruction} yields $\chita(\Cent(G)) \ge (n-1)+2 = n+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Refutation of the guiding conjecture]
Let $G$ be $d$-regular ($d \ge 2$) and not complete, $n=|V(G)|$. Then
$\chita(\Cent(G)) \ge n+1$. Regularity and non-completeness give $n \ge d+2$, so the
guiding value $d+3$ is correct at best only at the boundary $n=d+2$ and is strictly
too small whenever $n>d+2$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Each vertex has at least one non-neighbour, so $n-1-d \ge 1$, i.e. $n \ge d+2$ and
$n+1 \ge d+3$ (equality iff $n=d+2$). Combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[The pentagon]
For $G=C_5$ ($2$-regular, $n=5$) the guiding conjecture predicts
$\chita(\Cent(C_5)) = d+3 = 5$. Each vertex has two non-neighbours, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} gives $\chita(\Cent(C_5)) \ge 6 > 5$: five colours provably
do not suffice, contradicting the conjecture.
\end{example}

\section{A unified conjecture and small-case evidence}

Theorem~\ref{thm:indep} explains the complete case: its maximum-degree (original)
vertices are mutually non-adjacent, so Theorem~\ref{thm:obstruction} never applies
to such a pair --- exactly the hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}. For $K_{d+1}$,
$n=d+1$ and the expected value $d+2$ equals $n+1$. Both cases point to one value.

\begin{conjecture}[Unified exact value]
For every regular graph $G$ with $n=|V(G)| \ge 3$, $\chita(\Cent(G)) = n+1$.
\end{conjecture}

The results above establish $\chita(\Cent(G)) \ge n+1$ for all non-complete $G$
(and $\chit(\Cent(G)) \ge n$ in general). The matching upper bound --- an explicit
AVD total colouring with $n+1$ colours --- remains open and typically requires
Behzad/Vizing-style total-colouring constructions adapted to the two-layer
structure of $\Cent(G)$.

\begin{example}[An exact small value]
For $G=K_3$, the central graph $\Cent(K_3)$ has no non-adjacent pair to complete and
is isomorphic to the $6$-cycle $C_6$. An exhaustive search over proper total
colourings shows $\chita(\Cent(K_3)) = 4 = n+1$, consistent with the complete-case
value $d+2 = 4$.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Algorithm A (Central-graph construction).} From $G=(V,E)$, create tagged
vertices for each $v \in V$ and $e \in E$; add an edge between original $u,w$ iff
$\{u,w\} \notin E$, and between original $u$ and subdivision $e$ iff $u \in e$.
Complexity $O(n^2 + |E|)$.

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Algorithm B (Star-clique certificate).} Build $\Tot(H)$ by the
three adjacency rules; for a vertex $w$, extract its closed star and verify it is a
clique, certifying $\chit(H) \ge \deg_H(w)+1$. Complexity $O(|E(H)|^2)$.

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Algorithm C (Exact AVD total chromatic number).} For increasing
$k$, backtrack for a proper $k$-colouring of $\Tot(H)$ (vertices ordered by
descending degree, next-fresh-colour symmetry break), and test $S(u) \ne S(v)$ on
every edge $uv$; return the first successful $k$. Exponential worst case, exact.

\section{Applications and discussion}

Total colourings model conflict-free assignment of a shared resource to both units
(vertices) and interactions (edges); the AVD refinement additionally ensures
neighbouring units are distinguishable from their local colour footprint, relevant
to fault localisation and symmetry breaking. Central graphs are a canonical,
symmetric expansion of a base network and a standard testbed under
subdivision-and-completion. The lesson here is that this construction promotes
every original vertex to degree $n-1$, so the controlling parameter for
$\chita(\Cent(G))$ is the vertex count $n$, not the regularity $d$; a
parameter-specific conjecture phrased in $d$ is thereby invalidated for $n>d+2$.

Open problems: (1) prove the matching upper bound $\chita(\Cent(G)) = n+1$ for
non-complete regular $G$ by explicit colouring; (2) determine $\chita(\Cent(K_n))$
exactly, using the independence of Theorem~\ref{thm:indep}; (3) develop a reusable
total-colouring toolkit (total graph, AVD predicate, Total Colouring Conjecture);
(4) establish $\chit(H) \le \Delta(H)+2$ upper bounds for specific classes to
complement these lower bounds.

\section{Conclusion}

The AVD total chromatic number of the central graph of a regular graph is governed
by its number of vertices. The bound $\chita(\Cent(G)) \ge n+1$ is unconditional for
non-complete $G$, refuting the $d+3$ conjecture whenever $n>d+2$, while the complete
case is set apart by the independence of its maximum-degree vertices. These facts
point to the clean law $\chita(\Cent(G)) = n+1$ for all regular $G$, of which the
lower half is proved and the upper half is the principal task ahead.

